# man(1)

## NAME

man - display manual pages

## SYNOPSIS

    man [-L lang] [section] name...
    man -k keyword...

## DESCRIPTION

Displays the manual page for each name. Without a section, sections 1, 8, 5 and 7 are searched in this order.

When both standard input and standard output are terminals, a long page is shown one screen at a time. Otherwise the whole page is written without attributes, wrapped at 80 columns.

The language is taken from `-L`, or from the first non-empty of `LC_ALL`, `LC_MESSAGES` and `LANG`. For `ja_JP.UTF-8`, man tries `ja_JP.UTF-8`, `ja_JP`, `ja` and then English. `C`, `POSIX` and an unset value mean English. If a page has no translation, the English page is shown.

## OPTIONS

- `-L lang`: use this language
- `-k`: search the NAME line of every page for the keywords (case-insensitive)

## KEYS

- `space, f`: next screen (quits at the end)
- `Enter, j, Down`: next line
- `k, y, Up`: previous line
- `b`: previous screen
- `d, u`: half a screen down or up
- `g, G`: first or last line
- `q, Ctrl+C`: quit

## ENVIRONMENT

- `LANG, LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES`: the language of the pages
- `MANPATH`: directories to search, separated by `:` (default `/usr/share/man`)
- `MANWIDTH`: the line width (default: the screen width, or 80)

## FILES

- `/usr/share/man/manN/name.N`: English pages
- `/usr/share/man/LANG/manN/name.N`: translated pages

## EXIT STATUS

- `0`: success
- `1`: a page was not found, or `-k` found nothing
- `2`: usage error

## EXAMPLES

Show this page in Japanese:

    man -L ja man

Use Japanese for every page in this shell:

    export LANG=ja_JP.UTF-8

## NOTES

A page is a small Markdown file. Adding a directory such as `/usr/share/man/fr` adds a language. See `intro(7)`.

